decisions.detail:
Status. The declarations and code below are proposed D-TUI-SESSION1 additions. They are not shipped declarations.

Current source. Core.jet exports the existing typed events, tree, and widget pieces. Ui.rs has an internal program, backend, command machinery, and terminal guard. No public session or mouse constructor exists today.

The two choices use one complete task-list fixture. A and B change only the public owner call. The B fixture runs the real loop; it does not use synthetic `step` input.

### Common fixture

```jet
use core.tui as tui
use core.ui as ui

struct Model {
    tasks: [String]
    list_state: TuiListState
    table_state: TuiListState
    editor: UiTextEditorState
    saved: Bool
}

enum Msg {
    Event(TuiEvent)
    Saved
}

fn task_model() Model -> Model{
    tasks: [String]{"Write brief", "Review patch", "Ship change"},
    list_state: tui.list_state(),
    table_state: tui.list_state(),
    editor: ui.text_editor_state("Draft"),
    saved: false,
}

fn update(model: Model, message: Msg) (Model, TuiCommand<Msg>) !TuiError -> {
    if message == {
        .Event(event) -> {
            if event == {
                .Key(code, modifiers) -> {
                    if code == "enter" || code == "ctrl+s" {
                        model.saved = true
                        return (model, TuiCommand.Task(() -> Msg.Saved))
                    }
                    if code == "up" {
                        selected :: tui.list_state_selected(model.list_state)
                        model.list_state = tui.list_state_select(model.list_state, selected - 1)
                    }
                    if code == "down" {
                        selected :: tui.list_state_selected(model.list_state)
                        model.list_state = tui.list_state_select(model.list_state, selected + 1)
                    }
                    model.editor = ui.text_editor_reduce(
                        model.editor,
                        ui.text_editor_key(code, modifiers),
                    )
                    return (model, TuiCommand.None)
                }
                .Action(action) -> {
                    if action.kind == {
                        .Activate -> {
                            model.saved = true
                            return (model, TuiCommand.Task(() -> Msg.Saved))
                        }
                        .Scroll(vertical, _) -> {
                            selected :: tui.list_state_selected(model.table_state)
                            model.table_state = tui.list_state_select(
                                model.table_state,
                                selected + vertical,
                            )
                        }
                        else -> {}
                    }
                    return (model, TuiCommand.None)
                }
                else -> return (model, TuiCommand.None)
            }
        }
        .Saved -> {
            model.saved = true
            return (model, TuiCommand.None)
        }
    }
}

fn view(model: Model) UiNode -> {
    rows :: model.tasks.map(task -> [task, if model.saved -> "saved" else -> "open"])
    return ui.box([
        ui.text("Tasks"),
        tui.list_with_state(model.tasks, model.list_state),
        tui.table_with_state(["Task", "State"], rows, model.table_state),
        ui.text_editor(model.editor),
        ui.button("Save"),
    ])
}

fn adapt(event: TuiEvent) ?Msg !TuiError -> Msg.Event(event)

```

The proposed widget declarations used by this fixture are `TuiListState` state-taking list/table projections and the promoted #2468 `UiTextEditorState` reducer. They keep selection, offset, cursor, Unicode editing, paste, validation, and invalid-value retention in `Model`; `view` only projects state into the canonical tree.

### Public choice

A publishes `tui.run(model, update, view, adapt)` only. Its private implementation session owns first paint, the real TTY loop, commands, redraw, and cleanup. A does not publish a handle, `step`, `frame`, `state`, `stop`, `close`, `finish`, a driver, or a transport.

B publishes `tui.session(...)` and `TuiSession` only. The handle exposes the lifecycle below, including `run()` as a borrowed real-loop operation and `finish(^self)` as the only model extraction. B does not publish top-level `tui.run`.

