### Proposed public declarations

The following additions are proposed Jet notation. They are not current Core.jet declarations. `UiNodeId` comes from `core.ui`; the old `core.ui.host` declaration is rehomed, not aliased. `view` reads `M`; it never copies or consumes a generic model.

```jet
struct TuiResult<M> {
    model: M
    exit: TuiExit
}

struct TuiSession<M, Msg> {}

enum TuiExit {
    Close
    Interrupt
    Eof
    NonTty
    Stopped
}

enum TuiSessionState {
    New
    Active
    Stopped
    Closed
    NonTty
}

enum TuiStep {
    Ignored
    Updated
    Redrawn
    Exited(TuiExit)
}

#Error
enum TuiError {
    State(String)
    Input(String)
    Output(String)
    Update(String)
    Cleanup(String)
}

enum TuiMouseButton {
    None
    Left
    Middle
    Right
    Other(Int)
}

enum TuiMouseAction {
    Press
    Release
    Move
    Drag
    Scroll(Int, Int)
}

struct TuiMouse {
    column: Int
    row: Int
    button: TuiMouseButton
    action: TuiMouseAction
    modifiers: Int
}

enum TuiActionKind {
    Activate
    Named(String)
    Scroll(Int, Int)
}

enum TuiActionSource {
    Keyboard
    Mouse
}

struct TuiAction {
    node: ?UiNodeId
    kind: TuiActionKind
    source: TuiActionSource
}

enum TuiEvent {
    Key(String, Int)
    Resize(Size)
    Timer(String, Int)
    Io(String, [U8])
    Focus(Bool)
    Interrupt
    Close
    Mouse(TuiMouse)
    Action(TuiAction)
}

enum TuiCommand<Msg> {
    None
    Task(fn() Msg)
    Batch([TuiCommand<Msg>])
}

fn run<M, Msg>(
    model: M,
    update: fn(M, Msg) (M, TuiCommand<Msg>) !TuiError,
    view: fn(M) UiNode,
    adapt: fn(TuiEvent) ?Msg !TuiError
) TuiResult<M> !TuiError

fn session<M, Msg>(
    model: M,
    update: fn(M, Msg) (M, TuiCommand<Msg>) !TuiError,
    view: fn(M) UiNode,
    adapt: fn(TuiEvent) ?Msg !TuiError
) TuiSession<M, Msg> !TuiError

impl TuiSession<M, Msg> {
    fn start(&self) TuiSessionState !TuiError
    fn step(&self, event: TuiEvent) TuiStep !TuiError
    fn run(&self) TuiExit !TuiError
    fn frame(self) [String]
    fn state(self) TuiSessionState
    fn stop(&self) TuiExit
    fn close(&self) !TuiError
    fn finish(^self) TuiResult<M> !TuiError
}

fn mouse_event(
    column: Int,
    row: Int,
    button: TuiMouseButton,
    action: TuiMouseAction,
    modifiers: Int
) TuiEvent
```

`A` exports only `tui.run` and the shared event, command, result, mouse, action, and error types. It does not export `TuiSession`, `tui.session`, `start`, `step`, `run` as a method, `frame`, `state`, `stop`, `close`, or `finish`. The implementation may use a private session value.

`B` exports `tui.session`, `TuiSession`, and the lifecycle methods above. It does not export top-level `tui.run` or another managed facade. `session.run()` borrows the handle, runs the real loop, closes its guard, and returns only `TuiExit`; `finish(^session)` moves the final model. `close()` is borrowed, idempotent, and never returns the model.

The callback types are the common contract. Direct A calls use D-EFF2's normal effect flow-through. B stores callbacks in `TuiSession`; unknown-origin stored callbacks and `TuiCommand.Task(fn() Msg)` use D-EFF2's maximal effect set unless an existing `fn(…) -[E]>` bound is published. This proposal adds no effect-row syntax and does not hide authority.

### Shared tree and widget state

`core.ui` promotes the existing `JetUiNodeId` as canonical public `UiNodeId`. `core.ui.host` uses that same type and declares no alias or duplicate. `TuiAction.node` and layout hit regions use this identity.

The model owns widget state. Existing `TuiListState` remains the row selection and offset state for both lists and tables. Add these state-taking projections:

```jet
fn list_with_state(items: [String], state: TuiListState) UiNode
fn table_with_state(headers: [String], rows: [[String]], state: TuiListState) UiNode

#Error
enum UiTextEditorAction {
    Key(String, Int)
    Paste(String)
    Submit
    Cancel
}

struct UiTextEditorState {
    value: String
    cursor: Int
    width: Int
    error: ?String
}

fn text_editor_state(initial: String) UiTextEditorState
fn text_editor_key(code: String, modifiers: Int) UiTextEditorAction
fn text_editor_reduce(state: UiTextEditorState, action: UiTextEditorAction) UiTextEditorState
fn text_editor(state: UiTextEditorState) UiNode
```

Promote the existing #2468 value, cursor, width, and error reducer as `core.ui.UiTextEditorState`. The reducer preserves Unicode cursor movement, paste, validation, and invalid-value retention. These are the one list/table/editor mechanisms; the view only projects them into the one canonical `UiNode` tree.

Focus stays in the existing backend. Before paint, keep the last focused `UiNodeId`; after rebuilding the semantic tree, restore that identity. If it disappeared, choose the deterministic next neighbor, then the first focusable node. Resize repeats this rule. No session-local focus registry exists.

### Ownership, task, and exit contract

`TuiSession` owns `M`, callbacks, backend, presented layout, focus identity, command scope, and terminal guard. The view receives read access to `M`; update moves `M` into the callback and receives the next `M`. No generic clone or model copy exists.

`New` — `start()` on a TTY — Enter guard, build layout, present first frame, return `Active`.

`New` — `start()` without a TTY — Present one plain or labeled frame, return `NonTty` and enter `NonTty`.

`New` — `run()` — Call `start()`, then run the real loop; close on its exit and return its `TuiExit`.

`New` — `step()` — Return `TuiError.State`; do not call `adapt`.

`New` — `stop()` — Return `Stopped`; no guard or task exists.

`New` — `close()` — Succeed and enter `Closed`; no guard exists.

`Active` — `step(event)` — Process one event; `Close` or `Interrupt` shuts down and returns `Exited` with that exit.

`Active` — `run()` — Read until `Close`, `Interrupt`, `Eof`, or `stop`; close once and return the exit.

`Active` — `stop()` — Drain completed messages, cancel and join children, restore once, enter `Stopped`.

`Active` — `close()` — Perform the same shutdown, enter `Closed`, and preserve the exit.

`Stopped` or `Closed` — `step()` or `run()` — Return `TuiError.State`; `close()` still succeeds and is idempotent.

`NonTty` — `step()` or input read — Return `TuiError.State`; no read or block occurs.

Any unconsumed state — `finish(^self)` — Close if needed, preserve the stored exit or use `Stopped`, and move `M` exactly once.

A managed `tui.run` performs the same private transitions and returns `TuiResult<M>`. B's `session.run()` borrows the handle and returns only `TuiExit`; B's `finish(^self)` is the only model-extracting handle operation. `close()` is borrowed and never returns the model.

A normal `Task` result is one `Msg`; domain failures travel in that message. Every command is a child of the existing structured task scope. `Batch` starts children in listed order. There are no detached threads, public task scopes, or effect rows.

A completed child message that wins before shutdown is drained and delivered once through `update`. The boundary is explicit:

| Child message | Handling |
| --- | --- |
| Completed before stop or close begins | Queue once, deliver through `update`, and process its FIFO commands before restoration. |
| Completes during cancellation and before join returns | Treat as completed at the boundary, deliver once, then finish shutdown. |
| Completes after cancellation or after the session closes | Treat as late, reject it, and never call `adapt` or `update`. |

Shutdown requests cancellation, joins every child under the existing scope rules, and accepts no late message after the close boundary. Cancellation is cooperative where the existing scope requires it; the API makes no kill, abort, or crash promise.

A child or callback panic cancels and joins siblings, restores the guard once, and resumes the original panic. It is not converted to `TuiError`, ignored, or delivered as a fake message. Input, output, update, and cleanup failures attempt the same shutdown and return the first failure. A partial startup restores if guard entry happened.

`process.exit(code)` and `os.stop(code)` register the session cleanup with the existing D-FAIL-EXIT1 scope boundary. That boundary cancels and joins session children, restores the guard once, and preserves `code` before `atexit`. Host kill, abort, or crash is outside the cleanup promise.

### Event order and exact input outcomes

Start checks capabilities, enters the existing `JetTuiTerminalGuard` on a TTY, builds semantic layout, paints once, and presents before the first read. For ordinary events, normalize first, call `adapt`, apply each returned message with `update`, pump commands FIFO, and present one dirty semantic frame. Resize recomputes layout from semantic state.

One physical mouse record is atomic. The session snapshots the last presented layout and validates the cell. It calls `adapt(Mouse)`, invokes the snapshotted node target once, then calls `adapt(Action)` without reinvocation. It processes source then action messages and their FIFO commands, and presents one final frame. A source update cannot retarget the click because no new layout is consulted until that final paint.

Mouse capability unavailable — Drop before `adapt`; no action, update, or state change; continue.

Unsupported mouse encoding or action — Close with `TuiError.Input`; do not call `adapt`, then restore.

Malformed escape record — Close with `TuiError.Input`; `adapt` is not called.

EOF while a record is incomplete — Close with `TuiError.Input`; `adapt` is not called.

EOF with no partial record — Exit `TuiExit.Eof`; cancel, join, and restore.

Out-of-bounds cell — Drop before `adapt`; no action, update, or state change; continue.

Stale layout identity — Drop before `adapt`; no action, update, or state change; continue.

Valid in-bounds miss — Call `adapt(Mouse)`; derive no `Action`; process its optional message.

Valid in-bounds hit — Run the atomic mouse order above using the snapshotted `UiNodeId`.

Unknown key — Ignore; no update.

Mouse coordinates are zero-based cells. Press, release, move, drag, and scroll remain distinct. A valid press derives `Activate`; wheel derives `Scroll`. Keyboard bindings use the same `TuiAction` path.

### TTY, NO_COLOR, and non-TTY

A real TTY with `NO_COLOR=1` remains interactive. It enters raw mode, reads keyboard input, and accepts supported mouse input, but emits no ANSI color bytes. `NO_COLOR` changes color only. Mouse capability is detected independently of color.

A pipe, CI stream, or other non-TTY uses one one-shot plain or labeled frame and returns `TuiExit.NonTty`. It does not enter raw mode, set an alternate screen, move the cursor, read stdin, block, or emit progress rewrites. `NO_COLOR` on a pipe keeps that same one-shot rule. A real `TERM=dumb` TTY may use ASCII presentation but remains interactive with keyboard. Existing capability, width, Unicode, `FORCE_COLOR`, and `COLUMNS`/`LINES` policy remains the single policy.

Core.jet stays canonical. Generated checked signatures and runtime adapters carry the same event, state, frame, diagnostic, and cleanup meaning. Ui.rs may adapt its existing `JetTuiProgram`, `JetTuiBackend`, command machinery, and `JetTuiTerminalGuard`; it must not add a second tree, renderer, editor, focus registry, task mechanism, public driver, transport, or configuration object. ArgsProjection migrates its manual loop to the selected owner without changing #2468 behavior.

### Why A

A is recommended because most applications need one complete owner. B remains viable when callers need explicit lifecycle, stepping, or frame inspection. They are equal-task choices: both use the same Model, Msg, update, view, adapt, widget state, command scope, event order, and cleanup law.

The source evidence is limited to existing declarations and implementation seams. Runtime, PTY, cross-tier, and failure evidence remains future proof on #2919 and these cards; this preparation does not claim it.

