options.technical:
## B: marker-owned directives

B keeps the same `#FFI(asm)` body, raw bytes, and normalized descriptor. Optional marker fields provide complete constraints when declaration-first tooling needs them. Bare `#[..., FFI(asm)]` keeps A's exact short-form inference.

The proposed closed marker record is:

```text
inputs: { <parameter>: { constraint: <string> } }
inout: { <parameter>: { constraint: <string>, result: <path> } }
outputs: { <name>: { constraint: <string>, result: <path> } }
lateout: { <name>: { constraint: <string>, result: <path> } }
inlateout: { <parameter>: { constraint: <string>, result: <path> } }
temps: { <name>: { type: <Jet-type>, constraint: <string> } }
constants: { <name>: <Jet-comptime-expression> }
symbols: { <name>: { target: <Jet-symbol>, kind: "function"|"global" } }
labels: [<string>, ...]
clobbers: [<string>, ...]
clobber_abis: [<string>, ...]
options: [<string>, ...]
unwind: "forbid"
```

The complete marker for the shared fixture is:

```jet
#[Unsafe("U64 values are initialized; mul writes both Product128 fields; no memory or stack access is claimed; flags may change; x86-64 audit is complete"), FFI(asm,
    inout: { a: { constraint: "rax", result: "return.low" } },
    inputs: { b: { constraint: "reg" } },
    outputs: { high: { constraint: "rdx", result: "return.high" } },
    options: ["nomem", "nostack"], unwind: "forbid")]
fn wide_mul(a: U64, b: U64) Product128 -> {
    """mul {b}"""
}
```

The same `a=3, b=7` mapping produces `Product128{low: 21, high: 0}`. Sema rejects marker/body disagreement, duplicate destinations, missing outputs, generic outputs under `clobber_abi`, and unsupported modifiers. The body contains only instructions, placeholders, and declared labels.

B changes only where complete constraints are written. A and B share the short form, descriptor, target and CPU law, engine flow, diagnostics, safety gates, source map, and performance gate. B is valid when declaration-first tooling outweighs keeping facts beside the bytes.

