## A: target-qualified items and existing body dispatch

Proposed syntax, not shipped or executed:

```jet
#Target(Arch.x86_64)
#[Unsafe("audited x86-64 mul"), FFI(asm)]
fn wide_mul_x86(a: U64, b: U64) Product128 -> {
    """
    ; inout a -> return.low: rax
    ; in b: reg
    ; out high -> return.high: rdx
    ; options nomem, nostack
    ; unwind forbid
    mul {b}
    """
}

#Unsafe("dispatches audited target-specific assembly") fn wide_mul(a: U64, b: U64) Product128 -> {
    @if @build.target.arch == {
        "x86_64" -> wide_mul_x86(a, b)
        else -> portable_wide_mul(a, b)
    }
}

fn run() {
    #Unsafe("call the selected target implementation") {
        result :: wide_mul(3, 7)
        print(result)
    }
}
```

The full safe `portable_wide_mul` helper is the identical helper from D-FFI-ASM-COMPLETE1. It is included in the shared program, before this excerpt. Both arms return `Product128{low: 21, high: 0}`.

The proposed architecture gate applies only to the assembly item. The function-body `@if` remains a statement. On x86-64, the assembly declaration parses, common checks run, target checks run, and the item is available. On another target, the declaration parses and common checks run, but its target-specific assembly checks do not run, and the item is unavailable. The portable arm is checked and emitted.

A selected but unreachable x86 item is still target-checked and is not emitted. A false predicate takes `else`; a true predicate never falls back after an unavailable-item or invalid-assembly diagnostic.

