decisions.detail:
Option A explicitly selects first-party implementation using existing runtime IO. It is a proposal, not presumed authorization.

## Exact independent API choice

```text
consume(app: App, endpoint: String, queue: String, pattern: String, action: String, durable: Bool, prefetch: Int, acknowledgement: String) Void !TransportError
```

acknowledgement accepts only after-success. prefetch must be positive.

Invalid configuration fails before consuming.

TransportError distinguishes Config, Connect, Protocol, Action and AcknowledgementUnknown with secret-safe String messages.

The retained Nest pattern envelope maps to the existing typed App action.

Ack occurs only after action success.

Disconnect before or during ack permits redelivery and never promises exactly-once effects.

Durable=false is explicit for the fixture, not a changed default.

The receiver stops new admission on shutdown and closes its channel.

Existing App request authority and the durable idempotency ledger remain owners.

No hidden nack/requeue loop, retry count, dead-letter exchange or durable-queue default is selected.

Dependency disposition: neither option adds a Jet-linked third-party library, SDK, background broker, package installer, production endpoint or automatic download.

A is a first-party binding using existing runtime IO and the explicit protocol contract.

External broker servers are caller-selected services, never a stock default.

B leaves the complete peer adapter outside Jet.

Peer npm packages and versions are comparator fixture inputs only.

This is an explicit no-adoption boundary, not permission to choose an unnamed dependency later.

Any implementation that needs a new third-party dependency is outside this approved candidate contract.

The location choice remains D-NEST-RABBIT1. Its import alias in this example follows optional-package option A only for readability. The public member names, arguments, return carriers, error cases and lifecycle above are the API choice, regardless of the separately selected location. No placement choice silently approves this API.

A approves this exact contract. B definitively adds no Jet protocol API and uses the complete external peer program below. B is not a promise of a future binding. The earlier fixture text that asks for a separate API gate refers to this ballot; it does not create another postponed API decision. The exact A call below supersedes earlier illustrative call-return annotations where they differ.

## Complete A application

```jet
// PROPOSED optional first-party adapter; exact proposed module/API, NOT RUN.
use jet_rabbitmq as transport
use orders
fn run() {
    transport.consume(orders.app(), "amqp://orders:orders-local-only@127.0.0.1:5672",
        queue: "orders", pattern: "orders", action: "record-order",
        durable: false, prefetch: 1, acknowledgement: "after-success") ?? panic("RabbitMQ stopped; inspect delivery")
}
// Publish Nest event pattern orders with {"id":"order-41","sku":"book"}.
// One ledger row; disconnect before ack permits redelivery, not another charge.
```

## Complete B external route

```typescript
// NOT RUN; complete RabbitMQ server, broker, publisher and orders.ts below.
@EventPattern("orders")
async record(@Payload() order: Order, @Ctx() context: RmqContext) {
  await recordOrderOnce(order);
  context.getChannelRef().ack(context.getMessage());
}
// Queue orders; noAck:false; queueOptions.durable:false.
// Disconnect before ack: reconnect and handle the redelivery.
// Stable order-41 identity prevents a second ledger charge.
```

## Full shared source, current, peer and failure fixture

## Scope and preserved law

This is one distribution choice, not a new language mechanism. No keyword, sigil, Syntax.rs change or invariant exception is proposed.

D-DX-SERVERFN1=A/#2477 and D-ENDPOINT-SHAPE1=A/#2962 retain one checked operation and its public input, result, error, effects and policy facts.

The existing App and authoring direction remains binding. No adapter may create a second RPC registry, infer public endpoints from database rows or erase typed failures.

Existing request, connection and application ownership still govern cancellation and cleanup. A browser action keeps its existing auth and same-origin CSRF obligations.

This isolated non-browser fixture does not approve an authentication bypass for deployed services. Its plaintext loopback endpoints are not production security defaults.

The ratified Backend package remains installed by default outside core under #2799. B concerns only this added protocol adapter, not moving that existing bundle.

D-DX-QUEUE1=A/#2457 keeps the existing core.jobs lifecycle, bundled SQLite default and explicit configured database-provider override. These direct protocol consumers do not replace that queue.

D-NETSOCKET1 and D-NETTASK1 do not by themselves approve these compatibility APIs. Native sockets and native WebSocket keep their existing meaning.

I6 still requires an owner-approved external dependency and bridge pattern. I9 still requires one meaning across every applicable execution tier.

No placement choice approves an external library, version, new command, manifest field, public API or protocol default. The displayed imports and calls are exact proposals only.

The separate binding/API gate must resolve their types and failure mappings before implementation. Concrete npm and container pins below identify peer fixture targets, not Jet dependencies.

No provider was contacted and no program was compiled or run. A/B/C remain conditional designs; current interoperability remains unknown under the retained source evidence.

## One bound job

RabbitMQ 4.1.4 at amqp://orders:orders-local-only@127.0.0.1:5672; virtual host /; non-durable queue orders.

The ledger holds one 2500-cent charge row for order-41. The consumer acknowledges only after the action succeeds.

Disconnect after the ledger commit but before acknowledgement permits redelivery. Reconnect, handle the same order identity, then acknowledge without another charge.

The input is {"id":"order-41","sku":"book"}. The local ledger records a book charge of 2500 cents and returns recorded order-41.

The ledger is an authored HTTP fixture backed by a SQLite primary key. It represents a charge record only; no bank, payment account or external payment service is involved.

Replaying that identity returns the same receipt without inserting another row. This explicit business rule is not an adapter delivery guarantee or a new reliability model.

All five versions use the same ledger, input and protocol client. Run them separately in isolated directories, never as competing consumers against one fixture state.

Current is an honest unsupported attempt at that complete job. It does not pretend that a local function call proves broker or socket compatibility.

## Exact proposed adapter contract

RabbitMQ acknowledgement and the ledger commit are separate events. A publisher confirmation is a third event; it proves neither consumer success nor a ledger charge.

The proposed consume call selects one checked App action. The queue receives a Nest-compatible event envelope with pattern orders and data matching orders.Order.

The exact body is {"pattern":"orders","data":{"id":"order-41","sku":"book"}}. The AMQP messageId is order-41; the delivery tag remains a channel-local broker identity.

The proposed arguments select virtual host /, queue orders, pattern orders, durable false and prefetch 1. They are explicit fixture choices, not package defaults.

The proposed acknowledgement value after-success means manual acknowledgement on the receiving channel only after the action returns Ok. A failure does not acknowledge that delivery.

The call returns Unit !Err. A terminal connection failure returns Err; a closed channel cannot acknowledge through a later replacement channel.

A disconnect after ledger commit but before acknowledgement permits broker redelivery. Reconnect opens a new channel; the stable business identity remains order-41.

A returned ack call has no atomic transaction with the ledger. If the connection disappears, completion can be uncertain and the message may return.

This fixture deliberately uses a non-durable queue and non-persistent message. It promises no survival across broker restart or container replacement.

No automatic retry, poison-message policy or new dead-letter lifecycle is approved. Existing core.jobs law remains separate from this direct AMQP consumer.

The wild example uses EventPattern, not MessagePattern, because this job publishes an event without a reply. The manual acknowledgement obligation is unchanged.

## Fixture status and version boundary

Everything below is an authored, NOT RUN fixture. Retained snippets establish named primitives, not that these complete programs compile or interoperate.

Nest 12.0.1 package metadata is captured at commit 4c751c503bc753095f4b4f052e106f95218cc33f. The Nest documentation pages are unversioned live pages captured on 2026-09-11.

The Node target is 22.14.0. The ledger target is Python 3.12.10 with SQLite 3.45.3; these are explicit fixture targets, not captured installations.

TypeScript, Node types, supporting npm pins and container versions are concrete authored targets. Their availability, engine requirements and mutual compatibility were not established by retained captures.

[INFERENCE] Producer construction, observer calls, complete module wiring, container configuration and standard-library ledger glue complete the retained snippets. They are not quotations from the captured docs.

No additional source retrieval was performed. The orchestrator must reconcile uncaptured exact package/API details before describing this recipe as verified or using it for runtime proof.

Use a new empty working directory for each case. The file names below are literal fixture files, not an invented Jet setup schema.

