decisions.detail:
Option A explicitly selects first-party implementation using existing runtime IO. It is a proposal, not presumed authorization.

## Exact independent API choice

```text
Call law: open(endpoint: String, namespace: String, value_type: checked type operand) returns RemoteCache<V> !CacheError for that exact V. Members: get(key: String) V? !CacheError; set(key: String, value: V, ttl: Duration) Void !CacheError; remove(key: String) Void !CacheError.
```

RemoteCache is an owning connection handle, not a replacement for current Cache<K,V> or Query.

The checked value type supplies the existing codec; unsupported types fail before a request.

CacheError distinguishes Config, Connect, Encode, Decode and Transport with secret-safe String messages.

Endpoint, namespace and key are explicit Strings; TTL must be positive. get returns Some(false), Some(0) and Some("") for stored values.

Only absence, acknowledged removal or expiry yields None.

Transport failure is never a miss. set/remove complete only after primary acknowledgement; disconnect after a write has an unknown result and is not retried secretly.

One primary, no replica reads and no local copy define the fixture.

Existing Query and router state remain separate owners.

Closing the handle releases its connection without owning cached business truth.

Dependency disposition: neither option adds a Jet-linked third-party library, SDK, background broker, package installer, production endpoint or automatic download.

A is a first-party binding using existing runtime IO and the explicit protocol contract.

External broker servers are caller-selected services, never a stock default.

B leaves the complete peer adapter outside Jet.

Peer npm packages and versions are comparator fixture inputs only.

This is an explicit no-adoption boundary, not permission to choose an unnamed dependency later.

Any implementation that needs a new third-party dependency is outside this approved candidate contract.

The location choice remains D-NEST-REMOTECACHE1. Its import alias in this example follows optional-package option A only for readability. The public member names, arguments, return carriers, error cases and lifecycle above are the API choice, regardless of the separately selected location. No placement choice silently approves this API.

A approves this exact contract. B definitively adds no Jet protocol API and uses the complete external peer program below. B is not a promise of a future binding. The earlier fixture text that asks for a separate API gate refers to this ballot; it does not create another postponed API decision. The exact A call below supersedes earlier illustrative call-return annotations where they differ.

## Complete A application

```jet
// PROPOSED import/open; NOT RUN. Full two-process steps follow.
use jet_redis_cache as remote
fn run() {
    a :: remote.open("redis://127.0.0.1:6380/0", "orders", Bool)
        ?? panic("cache connection failed")
    _ :: a.set("order-41", false, ttl: 1s) ?? panic("cache write failed")
    value :: a.get("order-41") ?? panic("cache read failed")
    assert(value == Some(false))
}
// Process B opens the same endpoint and namespace; get is Some(false).
// After B removes order-41, A reads None after its acknowledgement.
// Expiry is None; connection failure is an error, never a cache miss.
```

## Complete B external route

```typescript
