decisions.detail:
Option A explicitly selects first-party implementation using existing runtime IO. It is a proposal, not presumed authorization.

## Exact independent API choice

```text
send_file(path: String, endpoint: String, allow: [String], max_records: Int, max_bytes: Int, timeout: Duration) ExportReport !ExportError
```

ExportReport has delivered: Int and failed: Int, both nonnegative.

ExportError has InvalidConfig(message: String) and Input(message: String).

Bounds and timeout must be positive; allow must contain only explicitly selected String field names.

Read and validate the bounded JSONL input before network side effects.

Reject malformed input, oversized input or invalid configuration through ExportError.

Each valid record sends only allowed fields as one application/json POST to the explicit endpoint.

Only HTTP 204 counts delivered; refusal, timeout and every other status count failed.

No redirect, background retry or cloud destination is implicit.

Local files remain unchanged.

The caller owns endpoint disclosure approval.

The separate sender cannot change either already completed business result.

The wire protocol is custom JSON POST /events, not OTLP or Observe.

Complete two-process trace-header fixture and corrupted-source quarantine are normative; no propagation or privacy runtime result is claimed.

Dependency disposition: neither option adds a Jet-linked third-party library, SDK, background broker, package installer, production endpoint or automatic download.

A is a first-party binding using existing runtime IO and the explicit protocol contract.

External broker servers are caller-selected services, never a stock default.

B leaves the complete peer adapter outside Jet.

Peer npm packages and versions are comparator fixture inputs only.

This is an explicit no-adoption boundary, not permission to choose an unnamed dependency later.

Any implementation that needs a new third-party dependency is outside this approved candidate contract.

The location choice remains D-NEST-EXPORTER1. Its import alias in this example follows optional-package option A only for readability. The public member names, arguments, return carriers, error cases and lifecycle above are the API choice, regardless of the separately selected location. No placement choice silently approves this API.

A approves this exact contract. B definitively adds no Jet protocol API and uses the complete external peer program below. B is not a promise of a future binding. The earlier fixture text that asks for a separate API gate refers to this ballot; it does not create another postponed API decision. The exact A call below supersedes earlier illustrative call-return annotations where they differ.

## Complete A application

```jet
// PROPOSED import/send_file; NOT RUN. Existing log files are below.
use jet_log_export as sender
fn run() {
    report :: sender.send_file("orders-api.jsonl",
        "http://127.0.0.1:4318/events", allow: ["trace_id", "service", "order"],
        max_records: 2, max_bytes: 4096, timeout: 100ms)
        ?? panic("invalid export configuration")
    assert(report.delivered == 1)
}
// Repeat for invoices-api.jsonl; receiver gets both 4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736/order-41 records.
// token is absent. Stop receiver: delivered=0, failed=1; no retry.
// This separate export process cannot change either service result.
```

## Complete B external route

sender.mjs:

```javascript
import { readFile } from 'node:fs/promises';
const path = process.argv[2];
if (path !== 'orders-api.jsonl' && path !== 'invoices-api.jsonl') {
  throw new Error('Pass one bound service JSONL path');
}
const content = await readFile(path, 'utf8');
if (Buffer.byteLength(content) > 4096) throw new Error('file exceeds 4096 bytes');
const lines = content.split('\n').filter(Boolean);
if (lines.length > 2) throw new Error('file exceeds two records');
const records = lines.map(line => {
  const { trace_id, service, order } = JSON.parse(line);
  if (typeof trace_id !== 'string' || typeof service !== 'string' ||
      typeof order !== 'string') throw new Error('invalid event fields');
  return { trace_id, service, order };
});
const report = { delivered: 0, failed: 0 };
for (const { trace_id, service, order } of records) {
  try {
    const result = await fetch('http://127.0.0.1:4318/events', {
      method: 'POST', headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },
      body: JSON.stringify({ trace_id, service, order }),
      signal: AbortSignal.timeout(100),
    });
    if (result.status !== 204) throw new Error(`HTTP ${result.status}`);
    report.delivered++;
  } catch { report.failed++; }
}
console.log(report);
```

Its file argument must be orders-api.jsonl or invoices-api.jsonl. Invoke it once for each file, after starting receiver.mjs. Those are real Node positional arguments, not a proposed Jet command. No command or receiver was run here.

## Complete peer emission

The peer uses Nest Logger plus Node file output, not an unnamed hosted exporter. Node 22.14.0 is the authored platform target. package.json uses real npm fields. @nestjs/common 12.0.1 is a retained pin; other listed versions are candidate fixture bindings, not tested compatibility claims.

