import { appendFileSync } from 'node:fs';
const service = process.env.SERVICE_ID;
if (service !== 'orders-api' && service !== 'invoices-api') {
  throw new Error('SERVICE_ID must be orders-api or invoices-api');
}
const event = { trace_id: '4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736', order: 'order-41', token: 'secret' };
const record = {
  trace_id: event.trace_id, service, order: event.order, token: '[redacted]',
};
appendFileSync(`${service}.jsonl`, JSON.stringify(record) + '\n');
new Logger(service).log(record);
console.log('order-41');
```

Run one process with SERVICE_ID=orders-api and a second with SERVICE_ID=invoices-api. The source rejects other values. Both emit the same selected event and return order-41. The same receiver.mjs and sender.mjs complete the peer export job. The raw event token is defined but never copied into the emitted record.

## Shared failure and restart sequence

1. Start the receiver, then run both event processes. Each local file contains one record. Run the selected sender once per file. Expected per-file report is delivered=1, failed=0; the receiver contains both records without token.

2. Stop the receiver. Run both services again in a fresh fixture directory, then invoke the sender for each new file. Expected report is delivered=0, failed=1. Each service result remains order-41. No retry, fallback destination, or hidden queue is allowed.

3. Restart the receiver without changing its endpoint. Local input files still exist. An explicit rerun may deliver them; an earlier ambiguous timeout can cause duplicates. The fixture accepts this limitation and does not imply exactly-once telemetry.

4. Supply an input file above 4096 bytes or above two records. The sender must reject it before sending. An invalid JSON record is also an input error. These limits are explicit fixture requirements, not newly approved global logging defaults.

## Retained Observe comparison

The captured @nestjs/observe 0.1.8 SDK documents createObserveModule, serviceId, forwardLogs, redaction keys, and trace correlation. Its documentation says hosted log forwarding needs Pro or above. It does not retain an OTLP or self-hosted ingestion endpoint. This is an evidence gap, not a claim that such products do not exist.

The exact two-process HTTP fixture therefore uses a source-defined receiver rather than invented Observe credentials or endpoint configuration. This choice does not select Node, Observe, or a custom protocol as Jet’s shipping dependency. It only makes the ownership comparison concrete.

## Source boundary

Current Log.rs was read directly for set_sink, otlp_file, redact, JSONL emission, and write-error handling. Its source bytes are identified in the evidence references. The retained Observe.rs and DevtoolsTelemetryPanel.rs locators lack captured bytes. Do not use them as implementation proof or infer feature absence.

The complete programs here are authored, NOT RUN. Node version compatibility, HTTP acceptance, privacy at the receiver, restart behavior, and bounded failures remain future execution cells. No source reading is presented as runtime proof.

## Existing law and independent gates

Keep core.log.set_sink and core.log.otlp_file. The current otlp_file implementation delegates to set_sink("jsonl", path). Its name does not establish OTLP wire encoding or network export. Existing local logs remain usable without any receiver, exporter, cloud account, or optional package.

D-OBSERVE-LIVE1=A remains binding. CORE-F023 owns core trace, recorder, and log interoperability. Application export composition stays separate. The retained inventory marks D-OBS3 and D-LOGFMT1 open; source comments are not ratification. This vote does not resolve those decisions or introduce a second trace store.

The proposed send_file call is an adapter binding over existing JSONL records, not a new generic event or trace API. It takes the bound file and HTTP destination, an allowlist, record and byte limits, and a timeout. Its result reports delivered and failed counts. Configuration, parsing, and local file errors use an error result.

This fixture sends one selected JSON object per request. It is explicitly not OTLP. Only HTTP 204 counts as accepted. Each network attempt ends within its 100ms timeout. Refusal, timeout, or another status increases failed. The adapter retries nothing and creates no extra spool or permanent export store.

The two input files remain the existing local log artifacts. Check their size and record count before sending anything. More than 4096 bytes or two records is a configuration or input error, not silent truncation. Reject malformed records before any partial send. Selection removes token rather than masking it in remote output.

A transport timeout leaves remote receipt uncertain even when failed increases. An operator rerun can duplicate a record; do not promise exactly-once delivery. delivered means HTTP acceptance by this fixture, not durable replication, indexing, or cloud retention. The receiver file can contain duplicates after a rerun.

The exporter runs as a separate process after each service returns order-41. Its failure cannot change that completed service result. Failed delivery stays visible in ExportReport; local files remain available. This is an explicit bounded batch fixture, not a new continuous-export default.

Trace 4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736 is correlation only. A caller-supplied trace header grants no identity, tenant access, or request authority. Every destination needs explicit operator opt-in. The loopback receiver and synthetic token are only fixture bindings; this ballot authorizes no network transmission or private-data disclosure.

A proposed module import and send_file binding need their own exact API gate. No OpenTelemetry SDK, hosted Observe plan, client library, cloud provider, package command, or default sink is approved here. Apply I6 to any later external dependency. Preserve all existing tier and safety requirements.

