Start worker.ts with STOP_AFTER_CLAIM=1, then run producer.ts. It exits with 17 after receiving the job. Restart Redis on the same volume. Start worker.ts with STOP_AFTER_CLAIM=0. Poll inspect.ts for completed, with a 120-second observation ceiling. A timeout is a failed or unknown cell, not completion.

The peer job id is invoice-41 and its result is order-41. The invoice file has the same bytes as Jet. The peer uses BullMQ stall recovery; it does not establish equality with Jet leases or retries. No captured source proves this crash sequence, exact client version, or bounded outage behavior.

## Source boundary

The retained Nest page proves forRoot connection fields and Processor/WorkerHost handler wiring. Its captured producer and registerQueue code are incomplete. The authored complete files fill bindings without claiming source or runtime proof. Source and version gaps remain explicit below.

The current queue fixture and default policy were read from the existing repository. This is source evidence on a changing tree, not a compiled or exercised program. Keep all execution and interoperability claims open.

## Binding law and independent gates

D-DX-QUEUE1=A/#2457 remains binding. The Prelude owns enqueue, lease, acknowledge, retry, dead-letter, wait, and status. SQLite at .jet/state/jobs.db remains the default. The explicit configured database provider remains available. #2799 consumes that lifecycle; #2919 retains the runtime obligation.

This is one additional broker-store adapter, not a second job policy or a BullMQ lifecycle inside Jet. It must implement the existing typed durable-store contract. Reject a Redis candidate that cannot do so. Do not weaken current database transactions to make this fixture pass.

At-least-once delivery can repeat handler effects. A crash after writing invoice-41.txt but before acknowledgement permits the same write again. This fixture rewrites identical bytes; it does not establish exactly-once external effects. Real invoice systems must enforce their own durable idempotency.

Existing declared authority, bounded retries, bounded history, job identity, request correlation, and payload privacy remain unchanged. Payload publication stays opt-in and bounded. An enqueue key is not the generated receipt id. Compare the returned receipt id across recovery; do not rename it invoice-41.

The proposed open call returns the existing core.jobs.JobQueue through its existing error carrier. It selects one explicit store endpoint and queue name. Its two String arguments are endpoint and queue. It adds no lease, retry, or dead-letter policy. The exact module and binding need separate API approval.

Redis 7.2.5 is an authored fixture target, not an approved Jet dependency or default. BullMQ is only the peer comparator. No Jet Redis client library is selected here. No manifest field, package installer command, cloud destination, or authentication policy is approved by a placement vote.

