decisions.detail:
## Bound common fixture

All code is NOT RUN. Two independent service processes are orders-api and invoices-api. Each receives the synthetic event trace_id=4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736, order=order-41, token=secret. Their business result is order-41. The fixture binds 4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736 explicitly to each process; it does not claim automatic cross-service context propagation.

Each process writes one local record, then exits. Use a fresh isolated directory so files contain no earlier events. orders-api writes orders-api.jsonl; invoices-api writes invoices-api.jsonl. Local token values are [redacted]. No raw secret is put into a local log record. The exporter removes the token field entirely.

The receiver is the complete receiver.mjs below, running on Node 22.14.0 at http://127.0.0.1:4318/events. It writes accepted records to received.jsonl. That file must start absent in a fresh fixture directory. This is a source-defined local provider, not an assumed cloud endpoint or OpenTelemetry collector.

## Current workload

orders-api.jet uses the existing logging surface:

```jet
use core.log as log
fn run() {
    log.set_sink("jsonl", "orders-api.jsonl")
    log.set_trace_id("4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736")
    log.info_fields("order.completed", [
        log.field("service", "orders-api"), log.field("order", "order-41"),
        log.redact("token"),
    ])
    log.flush()
    print("order-41")
}
```

invoices-api.jet is the same workload in the second process:

```jet
use core.log as log
fn run() {
    log.set_sink("jsonl", "invoices-api.jsonl")
    log.set_trace_id("4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736")
    log.info_fields("order.completed", [
        log.field("service", "invoices-api"), log.field("order", "order-41"),
        log.redact("token"),
    ])
    log.flush()
    print("order-41")
}
```

The source-backed expected local records include body=order.completed, their service name, trace_id=4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736, order=order-41, and token=[redacted]. The current formatter also supplies a timestamp; its value is runtime data, not a fixed golden. No records were emitted for this repair.

The remote part is not established by these calls. Source reading shows that set_sink selects a local file and otlp_file selects JSONL. The current write function discards file-open and write failures, so it does not supply the proposed delivery report. This is a concrete current limitation, not an invented diagnostic.

## Exact receiver for current workaround and every alternative

receiver.mjs is an authored fixture implementation. It checks the allowed field set, both service names, and the fixed trace and order values. No undefined sink setup remains. Reading this code does not prove network interoperability.

```javascript
import { createServer } from 'node:http';
import { appendFile } from 'node:fs/promises';
const expectedTrace = process.env.EXPECT_TRACE;
if (!/^[0-9a-f]{32}$/.test(expectedTrace || '')) throw new Error('EXPECT_TRACE is required');
const server = createServer(async (req, res) => {
  if (req.method !== 'POST' || req.url !== '/events') {
    res.writeHead(404).end(); return;
  }
  let body = '';
  for await (const chunk of req) {
    body += chunk;
    if (Buffer.byteLength(body) > 4096) { res.writeHead(413).end(); return; }
  }
  try {
    const record = JSON.parse(body);
    const keys = Object.keys(record).sort().join(',');
    if (keys !== 'order,service,trace_id' || record.trace_id !== expectedTrace ||
        record.order !== 'order-41' ||
        !['orders-api', 'invoices-api'].includes(record.service)) {
      res.writeHead(422).end(); return;
    }
    await appendFile('received.jsonl', JSON.stringify(record) + '\n');
    res.writeHead(204).end();
  } catch { res.writeHead(400).end(); }
});
server.listen(4318, '127.0.0.1');
```

Expected received.jsonl records, in either order:

```jsonl
{"trace_id":"4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736","service":"orders-api","order":"order-41"}
{"trace_id":"4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736","service":"invoices-api","order":"order-41"}
```

## Current workaround and complete peer sender

An application can author a separate Node sender for the current Jet JSONL files. The same sender below handles the peer files. It retains only trace_id, service, and order. This is an authored workaround candidate, not a shipped Jet exporter or captured Nest package capability.

sender.mjs:

```javascript
import { readFile } from 'node:fs/promises';
const path = process.argv[2];
if (path !== 'orders-api.jsonl' && path !== 'invoices-api.jsonl') {
  throw new Error('Pass one bound service JSONL path');
}
const content = await readFile(path, 'utf8');
if (Buffer.byteLength(content) > 4096) throw new Error('file exceeds 4096 bytes');
const lines = content.split('\n').filter(Boolean);
if (lines.length > 2) throw new Error('file exceeds two records');
const records = lines.map(line => {
  const { trace_id, service, order } = JSON.parse(line);
  if (typeof trace_id !== 'string' || typeof service !== 'string' ||
      typeof order !== 'string') throw new Error('invalid event fields');
  return { trace_id, service, order };
});
const report = { delivered: 0, failed: 0 };
for (const { trace_id, service, order } of records) {
  try {
    const result = await fetch('http://127.0.0.1:4318/events', {
      method: 'POST', headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },
      body: JSON.stringify({ trace_id, service, order }),
      signal: AbortSignal.timeout(100),
    });
    if (result.status !== 204) throw new Error(`HTTP ${result.status}`);
    report.delivered++;
  } catch { report.failed++; }
}
console.log(report);
```

Its file argument must be orders-api.jsonl or invoices-api.jsonl. Invoke it once for each file, after starting receiver.mjs. Those are real Node positional arguments, not a proposed Jet command. No command or receiver was run here.

## Complete peer emission

The peer uses Nest Logger plus Node file output, not an unnamed hosted exporter. Node 22.14.0 is the authored platform target. package.json uses real npm fields. @nestjs/common 12.0.1 is a retained pin; other listed versions are candidate fixture bindings, not tested compatibility claims.

