## Current workaround and complete peer sender

An application can author a separate Node sender for the current Jet JSONL files. The same sender below handles the peer files. It retains only trace_id, service, and order. This is an authored workaround candidate, not a shipped Jet exporter or captured Nest package capability.

sender.mjs:

```javascript
import { readFile } from 'node:fs/promises';
const path = process.argv[2];
if (path !== 'orders-api.jsonl' && path !== 'invoices-api.jsonl') {
  throw new Error('Pass one bound service JSONL path');
}
const content = await readFile(path, 'utf8');
if (Buffer.byteLength(content) > 4096) throw new Error('file exceeds 4096 bytes');
const lines = content.split('\n').filter(Boolean);
if (lines.length > 2) throw new Error('file exceeds two records');
const records = lines.map(line => {
  const { trace_id, service, order } = JSON.parse(line);
  if (typeof trace_id !== 'string' || typeof service !== 'string' ||
      typeof order !== 'string') throw new Error('invalid event fields');
  return { trace_id, service, order };
});
const report = { delivered: 0, failed: 0 };
for (const { trace_id, service, order } of records) {
  try {
    const result = await fetch('http://127.0.0.1:4318/events', {
      method: 'POST', headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },
      body: JSON.stringify({ trace_id, service, order }),
      signal: AbortSignal.timeout(100),
    });
    if (result.status !== 204) throw new Error(`HTTP ${result.status}`);
    report.delivered++;
  } catch { report.failed++; }
}
console.log(report);
```

