## Separate meaning and placement

This vote selects distribution and maintenance only. Each option uses the same proposed binding contract, inputs, protocol files, and caller outcomes.

The module names, package names, version 0.1.0, and bind functions below are explicit proposals. None is claimed to be a current Jet export.

No manifest schema is invented. A names a proposed first-party package. B names a proposed CoreLib module. C names an application module.

The imports use existing module-alias syntax. Exact package resolution and the new binding API need separate owner approval before implementation.

The peer package.json files configure Nest examples only. This vote selects no external Jet library, new command, production default, or remote disclosure.

The candidate bind consumes one checked App, validates the named public operations, and returns the same App with a protocol listener registration.

The existing App lifetime starts and stops that listener. A binding mismatch must reject before listening. There is no second application operation registry.

Binding and stream semantics remain owed under the separate semantic card. No placement can claim those semantics are already shipped.

D-DX-SERVERFN1=A and D-ENDPOINT-SHAPE1=A remain binding. Preserve typed public input, success, failure, capability, cancellation, and middleware facts.

Browser HTTP routes keep their same-origin policy. A non-browser protocol must not bypass capabilities or turn a transport status into successful application completion.

No new grammar, invariant exception, lifetime system, schema language, cloud service, or automatic publication is approved. No performance ranking is claimed.

## Exact gRPC contract

gRPC carries typed messages through a named service. The complete authored Protocol Buffers schema below fixes field numbers, method names, and streaming shape.

Save it as orders.proto beside package.json. Both server and client read that same file; no generated artifact is silently assumed.

```proto
syntax = "proto3";
package orders;
service Orders {
  rpc Create (CreateRequest) returns (CreateReply);
  rpc CreateMany (stream CreateRequest) returns (stream CreateReply);
}
message CreateRequest { string sku = 1; }
message CreateReply { string id = 1; }
```

The proposed bind maps CreateRequest.sku to NewOrder.sku. It puts the public String result into CreateReply.id. It never encodes StoredOrder.

Create is unary: one request and one reply. CreateMany is bidirectional: each admitted request uses the same create-order operation and emits one reply.

The success input is {"sku":"book"}. The decoded reply is {"id":"order-41"}; terminal status is OK, numeric 0.

The empty-sku unary call returns INVALID_ARGUMENT, numeric 3, with details sku_required and no reply. This maps the declared String failure explicitly.

The client deadline is five seconds. An already expired deadline returns DEADLINE_EXCEEDED, numeric 4. The candidate must reject admission after expiry.

The stream client sends one book request, reads one reply, and cancels. It expects CANCELLED, numeric 1, after that reply.

Cancellation prevents new admission after the server observes it. It does not erase the reply, revoke prior side effects, or prove unfinished work was interrupted.

Partial progress is explicit: one completed item remains completed. This fixture sends no second item; it cannot prove bounded buffering or cancellation under load.

The candidate must propagate the existing call lifetime and preserve bounded flow control. The retained Nest fragments establish neither behavior end to end.

These are local plaintext fixtures using 127.0.0.1:50051. No production TLS or authentication default is chosen. No credentials or remote data leave this host.

The proto, binding API, status map, deadline propagation, stream admission, and dependency versions remain separate semantic approval and proof work.

## Independent gRPC client: client.mjs

```javascript
import * as grpc from '@grpc/grpc-js';
import { loadSync } from '@grpc/proto-loader';
const definition = loadSync(new URL('./orders.proto', import.meta.url).pathname, { defaults: true });
const { orders } = grpc.loadPackageDefinition(definition);
const client = new orders.Orders('127.0.0.1:50051', grpc.credentials.createInsecure());
function create(sku, deadline) {
  return new Promise(resolve => {
    client.create({ sku }, { deadline }, (error, reply) => resolve({ error, reply }));
  });
}
const success = await create('book', Date.now() + 5000);
console.log(success.error?.code ?? grpc.status.OK, success.reply);
const rejected = await create('', Date.now() + 5000);
console.log(rejected.error?.code, rejected.error?.details);
const expired = await create('book', Date.now() - 1);
console.log(expired.error?.code);
await new Promise(resolve => {
  const stream = client.createMany({ deadline: Date.now() + 5000 });
  stream.once('data', reply => { console.log(reply); stream.cancel(); });
  stream.on('error', error => { console.log(error.code); resolve(); });
  stream.write({ sku: 'book' });
});
client.close();
```

