## Setup and outcome procedure

Use a new isolated fixture directory and broker project. Save the embedded files under their displayed names; do not reuse an existing topic or charge ledger.

The broker recipe uses Apache Kafka 3.9.1. Its single broker and replication factor one are local fixture settings, not production durability defaults.

Start the ledger and broker in separate terminals. After the broker accepts connections, create the topic, start one consumer, then publish once.

On a fresh topic, the first message has offset 0. The inspector should show partition 0 committed at 1 and one ledger charge row.

For the redelivery case, start the Nest consumer with HOLD_AFTER_RECORD=1. The defined fixture hook pauses after the ledger reply and before commit.

Publish once and inspect both systems. The row exists while this consumer has not committed its offset; stop only that fixture consumer.

Restart without HOLD_AFTER_RECORD. The same group resumes its uncommitted record and can commit 1 after the idempotent ledger call.

A/B/C use the identical fault boundary: stop after the ledger succeeds but before commit. Their future adapter proof must expose that boundary without adding public reliability policy.

Do not treat a broker publish response as consumer completion. For an uncertain commit, inspect the group offset and ledger separately.

