Start the ledger and broker in separate terminals. Once RabbitMQ is ready, start one consumer and publish the defined event once.

After successful handling, the ledger contains one charge row. Queue inspection should settle at ready 0 and unacknowledged 0.

For the redelivery case, start the Nest consumer with HOLD_AFTER_RECORD=1. The defined fixture hook pauses after the ledger reply and before acknowledgement.

Publish once and inspect both systems. The ledger has one row while RabbitMQ retains an unacknowledged delivery; stop only that consumer.

Restart without HOLD_AFTER_RECORD. The requeued delivery reaches the same operation, which returns the same receipt, then acknowledges on its new receiving channel.

A/B/C use the same fault boundary and ledger. Their future adapter proof must stop before acknowledgement without turning the fixture hook into public policy.

