decisions.explainer:
:: names a value. *cell takes its address; p.* reads through it. The pointer cannot outlive cell. #Unsafe("reason") reviews the code inside its braces, not future callbacks. assert no_alias and assert valid_ptr,aligned are expert operation claims, not runtime tests.

An effect row lists possible work. -[]> says no listed work escapes; -[Unsafe]> grants no permission. Containment, or discharge, removes locally executed Unsafe from the outward list. Latent work is a callback's future body. A ceiling is the most work an interface permits. Joining rows keeps work possible on any path. Unknown callback work cannot be erased.

Proposed runner examples:
```jet
fn safe_value() -[]> Int { 7 }
fn run_safe(cb: fn() -[]> Int) -> Int { cb() }
fn run_unknown(cb: fn() -> Int) -[via cb]> Int {
    #Unsafe("review local work; callback may do more") { cb() }
}
#Unsafe("caller must review entry to this expert routine")
fn expert_value() -> Int { 7 }
fn reviewed_value() -> Int {
    #Unsafe("expert_value was reviewed for this wrapper") { expert_value() }
}
// All: run_safe(safe_value) returns 7
// A: expert_value has Unsafe and unsafe-call bit
// B: expert_value has unsafe-call bit but no Unsafe row
// C: expert_value and reviewed_value both have Unsafe
// A/B: run_safe(sample) and run_safe(reviewed_value) return 7
// C: those callbacks exceed run_safe's empty ceiling
// All: ungated expert_value() refuses lexical permission
// All: run_unknown retains unknown via-cb work
// Declaring run_unknown -[]> refuses unresolved callback work
```

Constructing a callback does not run it. A refuses returning fn()-[Unsafe]> as fn()-[]>. A known explicit Unsafe callback can execute inside A's reviewed boundary; unknown work cannot be relabelled safe. These are proposed acceptance outcomes, not exercised results.

