options.detail:
Put #Post(condition, "message") on the line directly above an #Unsafe block. It reuses function #Post's spelling, purity rule, E3005 error, cleanup behavior and proof removal. A false result stops the program with E3005 and your message, and cleanup still runs during that stop, as it does for a failed function #Post today. Because a block is not a function, when it runs is defined here.

When it runs: every normal finish of the block, which means falling off the end or leaving with break, continue or return. A return (including one made by ?) is judged exactly as the function's own #Post judges that returned value. In a function that declares Option or Result, return None and return Err(...) are ordinary answers, so the block check runs. In a function whose failure is implicit, return Err(...) is a failure exit.

When it does not run: failure exits, panic and cancellation. That first problem keeps being the one reported, so a check can never hide a real failure.

Order on a return: the block's #Post clauses run first, in source order, then the function's own #Post. The first false clause stops the program; later clauses do not run.

Examples. Each block below has #Post(cell == 7, "cell must still be 7") above it, and cell :: value.

// declared Option: None is an ordinary answer
fn pick(value: Int) -> Int? { ... if cell != 7 -> return None ... }
pick(8) // stops: E3005 cell must still be 7

// declared Result: Err is an ordinary answer
fn checked(value: Int) -> Int Rejected! { ... if cell != 7 -> return Err(Rejected.Bad) ... }
checked(8) // stops: E3005 cell must still be 7

// implicit failure: Err is a failure exit
fn quiet(value: Int) -> Int { ... if cell != 7 -> return Err("not 7") ... }
quiet(8) // the caller gets Err("not 7"); the block check is skipped

// block and function both have #Post
#Post(result > 0, "answer must be positive")
fn both(value: Int) -> Int { ... return observed - 7 ... }
both(8) // stops: E3005 cell must still be 7 (the block check runs first)
both(7) // stops: E3005 answer must be positive

Cost: one evaluation of the condition per normal finish. For small number comparisons that is a few machine instructions. For a huge whole number (Int has no size limit) the comparison takes time in proportion to its digits, like any other comparison of that number. A condition that jet prove proves always true has its check removed, as for function contracts. The existing per-module contract opt-out still applies.

